\f1\fs22 Circumstances deprived Sarah of time for processing Kleoboulos\'92s obscene suggestion. And, by 
\f2\i circumstances
\f1\i0 , we mean the appearance of Agent Jones. On seeing Sarah sharing a booth with a sweat bag in an expensive suit, attended by three old geezers in pyjamas, he knew with a certainty, some shady transaction was going down. He pulled his weapon, trained it at Sarah, and shouted, \'91Freeze!\'92 Unfortunately, the karaoke was louder than his shout. In any case, it\'92s doubtful an Elvi, once on stage, would ever finish a performance before the fat momma sings. For the Elvi on stage, freezing was no option.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A squad of PAPER agents infiltrated and took up positions by exits and corners. Agent Jones shot a chandelier to shards. Partygoers screamed. Those under the exploding light-fixture ducked and jumped. In the dimmer light, it was unclear if any were hit or cut.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With particle pistol extended and aimed, Agent Jones swiftly reconnoitred the room, found the sound system booth, and adjusted the volume with angry lead. \'91Everyone will freeze!\'92 he repeated now everyone could hear him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony rose from behind his bottles and teetered defiantly. In his periphery, Agent Jones saw Tony immediately, which overloaded his axonal circuits. His particle pistol mechanically alternated between aiming at Tony and Sarah. Agent Jones nodded; a pair of agents apprehended Tony. Tony kept very still, lessons learned from bitter experience. Only his eyes fizzed with the rage he felt. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hero Johnny hid under the table. The sages stood in a line with backs to Sarah, forming a dubious barricade of varicose veins and arthritic joints wrapped in shooting stars and cutesy planets.
\f3\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \

\f1\fs22 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For what he must say and do, Kleoboulos drew a long and stately breath, and stepped once. \'91Dear, young Ajent. I wonder if you could consider for a moment, the enormity of the storm you find yourself in.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones considered the enormity of why he hadn\'92t blown the head off the string-bean garden gnome yet. It must have been because the gnome knew his first name and, by the way he pronounced 
\f2\i Ah-\ul zhent
\f1\i0 \ulnone , knew how to spell it as well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos, sensing the reptilian gearbox of Agent Jones\'92 brain was in neutral, took another slither of a step. \'91What are you here for, young Ajent?\'92 Again with the pronunciation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones\'92 eyes momentarily blanked. Kleoboulos smiled beneficently; he had been practicing beneficent smiles\'97chortles, guffaws, stares, and even snarls too\'97since Jamison Hardy was a boy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I will have something she took from me back,\'92 Agent Jones answered, like a schoolboy before the head mistress.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos\'92 eye twinkled, also from much-practiced beneficence. \'91What she has is neither yours nor hers.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I will have the disc!\'92 Agent Jones snapped his pistol\'92s aim, which had been tracking slowly for the floor, at Kleoboulos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You will not have the disc,\'92 said Kleoboulos. \'91It is much too dangerous in the hands your masters at Preemptive Reciprocity and Proactive Euthanasia
\f2\i .\'92\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> The percussive sound of cocking triggers and cracking fingers rang out as all the PAPER agents aimed at Kleoboulos.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then I will have her death,\'92 Agent Jones snarled.
\f2\i  
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You don\'92t want to kill her,\'92 Kleoboulos, wheedled. \'91You want to 
\f2\i please
\f1\i0  her. You want to 
\f2\i worship
\f1\i0  her.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony, who was inching to a position where he may be more useful if the situation escalated, almost choked on the heart in his mouth, and swallowed hard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Ajent Jones went limp with the force of Kleoboulos\'92 words. Kleoboulos tasted he was within moments of neutralising young Ajent, released a gloating smile. Embarrassed by his ill-discipline (he\'92d never been taught to gloat beneficently), he reconquered his face. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos may have indeed neutralised the threat that night, if not for the sound of twenty bulls in five china shops reverberating from deeper parts of the club.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the tax is that?\'92 With a wave, Agent Jones sent several agents to investigate, while he grabbed a waiter in a choke-hold and pointed a particle pistol at her head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos shut his eyes and exhaled the breath of long sufferance. \'91It is the sound of several Elvi blasting all the toilets to smithereens. I suggest you recall your agents.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones advanced on Kleoboulos and aimed at Kleoboulos. \'91What\'92s that you say?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The good thing about beneficent expressions is they betray no fear. Kleoboulos showed no fear. \'91Have you ever read a Warranty card for an ELVIS Entertainment and Hospitality Unit? I suppose not. You\'92re too young to remember the emancipation of the Elvi, let alone when a company called Grayling Corporation produced and sold many thousands of ELVIS clones into hospitality and domestic servitude. Pity: there\'92s one particular warranty instruction that you would have done well to heed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones, regaining his sadistic mojo, ground the pistol barrel into the waiter\'92s temple. \'91Is that so? And which instruction was that?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Never get an ELVIS angry.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91An ELVIS? What, you mean these pathetic sideshow karaoke clowns?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos sighed, realising there\'92d be no neutralisation after all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones had ignored another fundamental piece of advice when infiltrating The Ghetto: always assume the Elvi are packing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Any waiter and performer who was carrying (which it to say, all of them) snapped their pieces from leather jacket and jumpsuit pockets. The PAPER agents were well out-gunned. An Elvi advanced on Agent Jones. Knowing he was licked, Agent Jones put his hands in the air. Reflective flashes from the Elvi\'92s jewellery and accessories made him blink. Among everything else, she wore a Star of David and a crucifix around her neck, and a metallic broach, which inhabitants of other universes would recognise as a Bureau of Narcotics and Dangerous Drugs badge, identical to the one Richard Nixon gave Elvis in 1970.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You should leave,\'92 Narc Elvis told Agent Jones.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91OK, OK, we\'92re leaving.\'92 Agent Jones had difficulty processing how to back down. Like many men in many generations in many universes, he\'92d couldn\'92t leave without delivering a parting shot. \'91No need to get strung out, man.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What did you just say to me?\'92 Narc Elvis asked with cold, barely contained violence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones, believing he was leaving on a high note, turned to go.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hey!\'92 Narc Elvis grabbed Agent Jones\'92 shoulder and spun him around. \'91No one ever walks out on me when I\'92m talking.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sure \'85 sorry \'85\'92 Agent Jones aggressively swung out of the Elvi\'92s grasp.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What did you say to me?\'92 Narc Elvis repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I just said\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You think I\'92m strung out? You think I\'92m strung out, huh?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, no\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well let me tell you, I\'92ve never been strung out in my life, except on music.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones should have read the warranty card. \'91Well, if by 
\f2\i music
\f1\i0 , you mean this karaoke shit you people\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I oughta break your goddamn neck, you son of a sow. I oughta pull your goddamn tongue out by the roots. Thank you very much.\'92 Narc Elvis delivered a swift karate blow to Agent Jones\'92 neck and sent him down like a sack of deep-fried pickles.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The other PAPER agents were too slow to react. The Elvi shot out the remaining chandeliers and wall lights. Tourists screamed and hid under tables. In the dim of the table lights, the Elvi grabbed handfuls of balls from pool tables. They hurled them at PAPER agents and mirrors with equivalent zeal. They raised chairs and splintered them over the heads of anyone within reach who had a head.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The PAPER agents had a snowball\'92s chance in Blue Hawaii.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Let\'92s back up a little, and return our story to Sarah \'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As we have told already, Agent Jones snapped his particle pistol at Kleoboulos. \'91I will have the disc!\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At this point, Sarah slid the Gyges Disc into her bag. The thing glowed with sinister hot light, as if the warmth of her touching it and protecting it from Johnny had brought it to life. She considered dropping it on the floor and kicking it as far away as her heels would allow. But the prospect of losing it gripped her heart in a vice. There it was glowing in her open bag, as ruddy and hale as a baby in a manger, and all at once, Sarah recognised the insecurity of leaving it in her bag. It would be too easy for Agent Jones to rip it from her. She retrieved the disc\'97first making sure everyone\'92s attention was adhered on Agent Jones\'92 pistol barrel\'97and popped in her bra. It felt exhilarating, the way the disc seemed to caress her contours.\
